for most people going to the movies is a  surefire form of stress relief one  that's specifically intended to distract  us from the turmoil of our own lives for  at least an hour or two of course that  can occasionally backfire because some  films just won't allow you to switch off  your brain and check out of the world  for even one single minute that's not  always a bad thing though in fact some  of the best films out there are good  simply because they challenge our hearts  and minds here are some of the most  stressful movies you'll still want to  watch 7 when 7 made its way to theatres  is back in the fall of 1995 the world  was simply not ready but Dave adventures  relentlessly bleak Symphony of Sorrows  is still a masterpiece that continues to  serve as a bizarre gory mirror for moral  and societal excesses the film opens on  a dour note and only gets more desolate  desperate and stressful with every  moment that follows still 7 is so  meticulously constructed obsessively  executed and skillfully acted by its  leads that even at its most gruesome  it's almost impossible not to want to  watch it all unfold whether it's because  of sevens obsessive nature it remains  one of the most rewatchable movies ever  made  no matter how difficult it is mother  speaking of directors who like to push  buttons Darren Aronofsky is another  director known to break boundaries but  nothing in his riveting collection could  have prepared audiences for the brain  bomb that was his 2017 offering mother  set in a tranquil secluded country home  the film follows a struggling poet and  his young wife as they try to write and  remodel their home that peaceful  existence is disturbed with the arrival  of unexpected guests things quickly get  out of hand as mother propels itself -  it's shocking but inevitable finale the  moments that lead us there come with a  constant feverish upping of the ante  watching mother is an emotionally  draining affair which might explain the  movies box office problems but like the  best of Aronofsky's films the anxiety  and discomfort caused by watching it is  still totally worth it  buried for much of his early career  thought of taking Ryan Reynolds  seriously as an actor just seemed  laughable but many film goers opinions  of Reynolds as an actor were forever  changed after seeing his nerve-wracking  2010 micro budget thriller buried the  whole film takes place in a coffin  buried somewhere in the Iraqi desert and  Reynolds is the sole actor and yes he's  buried inside the coffin if you're  claustrophobic sitting through this  movie is a lot like subjecting yourself  to a ninety five minute anxiety attack  and even if you're not afraid of  enclosed spaces buried is still tough to  tangle with but if you can make it  through the cramped space of the film  it's a first-rate thriller with a few  clever twists some surprisingly  insightful commentary and an impressive  dramatic performance from Ryan Reynolds  who doesn't snidely snicker or break the  third wall even once under the skin  sometimes it's hard to pinpoint exactly  what it is about a movie that stresses  you out but in the case of Jonathan  Glaser's under the skin it's quite  literally everything  the film's photography music acting  sound design and editing are designed  with the single goal of keeping viewers  on edge the result is a challenging  deeply disturbing movie that you don't  really watch as much as you experience  at the same time though it's also a  profoundly engaging ambitious picture  that hypnotizes viewers into entering  its darkness as it examines complex  topics of identity extraterrestrial  morality and what it means to be human  at the center of that exploration is the  single weirdest and possibly strongest  performance of Scarlett Johansson's  career the film works best if you know  as little as possible going in but  Watchers should be warned that if you  feel uneasy while watching the flick  that just means it's working enemy  deneva loves doppelganger thriller enemy  is adapted from a novel by experimental  novelist josé saramago and follows an  average man whose middling life is  thrown into flux when he spots his exact  double in a movie what follows is a  study of identity and modern isolation  that finds Jake Gyllenhaal in top form  playing dual roles and Vilna of  beginning to tease the daring style  that's become his trademark in the years  it's also worth noting that spiders  figure prominently in the film which  means there's a fair amount of arachnid  angst involved in watching me more  importantly though the film is also rife  with metaphorical webs that the director  uses to pull viewers further into the  sticky deadly center enemy is one of  those rare movies that's distressing  enough to just watch and even more so  after the credits have rolled and you're  stuck trying to figure out what the hell  just happened  bug William Friedkin directed one of the  most unnerving films ever made with  2006's buck but odds are you've never  heard of it bug features one of the  finest performances of Ashley Judds  career and a breakout turn from the  great Michael Shannon perhaps one of the  reasons bug hasn't quite broken any  box-office records is that it's hardly a  feel-good affair the bulk of the film is  set inside a dingy hotel room and  follows a damaged woman who seems to be  on the verge of a fresh start after  meeting a kind' war vet when her new pal  turns out to be one deeply unhinged  individual what follows is a terrifying  descent into madness Friedkin keeps the  action taught throughout making the  proceedings extra stressful by using  expressionistic lighting shooting his  stars and unflattering close-ups  featuring a handful of body horror  scenes that will definitely make your  skin crawl bug is essentially the  cinematic equivalent of poking at a raw  nerve for a couple of hours but it's  well worth the pain  [Music]
 

